capability fs/space  the bytes free and the size of the volume a path stands on
  heroes  fs.space
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  shutil.disk_usage, os.statvfs
  note    three figures, as `os.statvfs` tells them apart: `available` the bytes this user may write (`f_bavail`, Python's `disk_usage().free`), `free` those free to any user with the system's reserve (`f_bfree`; `GetDiskFreeSpaceExW`'s `lpTotalNumberOfFreeBytes`), `total` the size; Python's `used` is `total` less `free`, so it is not a field of its own. Measured 2026-11-24 against Python 3.15.2's `shutil.disk_usage` and `os.statvfs` on this Mac (994662584320 total, `used` 902470672384 equal to `total` less `f_bfree`), `df -B1` in the Linux arm64 image, and `Win32_LogicalDisk` on the Windows box

capability fs/volumes  every volume mounted on the machine listed: where it is mounted, what is mounted there, its file system, its label, its size and free bytes, and whether it is read only, on another machine, removable or kept from the file manager
  heroes  fs.volumes
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    added 2026-11-24 (gap G08). None of Go 1.27.4's `os`, Rust 1.100.0's `std::fs` or Python 3.15.2's `os` and `shutil` lists the mounted volumes on every system (Go's `syscall.Getfsstat` is Darwin's and the BSDs' call; Python's `os.listdrives`, `os.listmounts` and `os.listvolumes` are Windows' alone, read in CPython 3.15.2's `Doc/library/os.rst` this session), so the yardsticks are psutil's `disk_partitions` with `disk_usage`, Java's `FileStore` and .NET's `DriveInfo`, none installed here; the answers were held to the systems' own tools instead (`hero_volumes.h`). Darwin: `getfsstat` with `MNT_NOWAIT`, so a share that does not answer holds no call, the label `getattrlist`'s `ATTR_VOL_NAME`, not asked of a volume on another machine; this Mac's 11 mounts were `mount`'s 11 in its order, each size `df -k`'s, each label `diskutil info`'s (`Macintosh HD`, `VM`, `Data`, `Recovery`, devfs none), `/` read only and every `nobrowse` mount hidden. Linux: `/proc/self/mountinfo`, its escapes read, each mount asked `statvfs`, network as systemd v262.2's `fstype_is_network` (its `@network` set and four names, read this session), removable as the kernel's `removable` of the disk under it (`/sys/dev/block`), the label as udev links it in `/dev/disk/by-label`; in the Linux arm64 image the 24 mounts were `findmnt`'s 24 in its order and the sizes `df -B1`'s. Windows: every drive letter and then every folder a volume is mounted on (`FindFirstVolumeW`, `GetVolumePathNamesForVolumeNameW`), each asked `GetVolumeInformationW`, `GetDiskFreeSpaceExW` and `GetDriveTypeW`, the system's dialog for a drive with no medium kept away; on the Windows box `C:\` alone, its volume name, `NTFS`, size and free bytes as `Get-Volume`, `Win32_LogicalDisk` and `mountvol` gave them, the EFI volume with no mount folder left out. A mount the system will not tell the size of is left out, never made up, and a mount folder that is not UTF-8 fails `not_text` as a folder's entry does. `hidden` is Darwin's `MNT_DONTBROWSE`; Linux and Windows mark no volume so, and their file managers' choice of what to show (GIO's guesses, Explorer's policies) is theirs. Unrun: a removable medium, a network share and a folder-mounted volume on any of the three, none being on our machines; a Linux machine with udev (the image has no `/dev/disk/by-label`)

