capability fs/write-atomic  a file written whole in one step that no reader and no crash of the machine sees half done
  heroes  fs.write_atomic_bytes, fs.write_atomic_text
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    none of the three offers it whole, and each program writes it from parts (Go's os.CreateTemp, File.Sync and os.Rename; Rust's File::sync_all and fs::rename; Python's tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile with delete=False, os.fsync and os.replace, each in its own block): this does those parts in that order, a temporary beside the target made new, written, put on the storage (F_FULLFSYNC on Darwin), closed and renamed over the target in one step, then the folder put on the storage on POSIX, the rename written through on Windows (MoveFileExW with MOVEFILE_WRITE_THROUGH, unrun until the box runs it). A link at the path is replaced itself, as os.Rename and os.replace do; the replaced file's permission bits are kept, which none of the parts does unasked, so a 0o600 file stays private; the owner is not kept. Go's renameio and Python's atomicwrites, which do the same, are outside the standard libraries

capability fs/sync-dir  a folder's entries put on the storage, so a name made, renamed or removed in it outlives a crash
  heroes  fs.sync_dir
