  note    no address of a map reaches the program: bytes are copied out and in at an offset the call names, an io.ReaderAt's shape (Go's golang.org/x/exp/mmap, outside the standard library, as is Rust's memmap2), every range checked against the map's size before room is made, so a count from outside never makes a large buffer. A read past the end fails `invalid` where Python's slice is cut short (measured: `m[3:9]` of five bytes answers two) and an io.ReaderAt answers fewer bytes with an error. An empty file is a map of size 0, every system refusing to map no bytes, where Python refuses the file (measured: ValueError, `cannot mmap an empty file`). `copy` is Python's ACCESS_COPY, MAP_PRIVATE and PAGE_WRITECOPY. A file is mapped whole at its size then; Python's offset and length, which map a part, are not taken. On POSIX another program may cut a mapped file and the system then ends a process touching past the cut (SIGBUS): each copy asks the file's size first and fails `failed` past it, which narrows the window and cannot close it; Windows refuses the cut. The Windows arm is unrun until the box runs it

